const e=JSON.parse(`[{"id":"BE_E7_001","exercise":"exercise_7","type":"best_essence","passage":"Individual angst is all very well, but as a collective we human beings rate ourselves pretty highly. We know we are unique, fabulous and important because we tell ourselves so all the time. And, although the epithet 'terminal uniqueness' was coined in reference to alcoholism, it could also apply quite generally. And we do, of course, possess some interestingly distinguishing features. For example, we are the only species that extracts resources from the ground via mining or quarrying. Unfortunately, it is precisely this kind of unique behaviour that tends to get us into one fine mess after another, as catalogued by the recently released Millennium Ecosystem Assessment. An audit of the world's ecosystem services, it reveals that 60 per cent of the world's ecosystem services are being used unsustainably and/or being degraded. Failing to curb our enthusiasm for just about all of the Earth's resources, including fresh water, fish stocks and virgin forest, means that between 10 and 30 per cent of the world's species face extinction. The human ego, it seems, is inversely proportional to levels of biodiversity.","options":{"A":"Despite our anxiety we are unique because we can mine and quarry and we tell ourselves how important we are. Our 'terminal uniqueness' leads us to destroy the world around us leading to extinction of species that is 60% of the ecosystem.","B":"Though we are unique in some ways, we think too highly of ourselves and are probably 'terminally unique'. Our unique behaviour has got us into a mess as revealed by the Millennium Ecosystem Assessment. Our self-conceit has led us to overexploit resources leading to the extinction of a number of species.","C":"'Terminal uniqueness' is what makes us individually anxious but collectively unique. Our unique features are listed in the Millennium Ecosystem Assessment. Our enthusiasm for exploiting resources has led to the extinction of 60% of the species on Earth.","D":"The Millennium Ecosystem Assessment shows that we have made ourselves unique through mining and quarrying. But our uniqueness has become a threat for our environment as we overexploit resources which leads to extinction."},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"Though we are unique in some ways, we think too highly of ourselves and are probably 'terminally unique'. Our unique behaviour has got us into a mess as revealed by the Millennium Ecosystem Assessment. Our self-conceit has led us to overexploit resources leading to the extinction of a number of species.","option_analysis":{"A":{"fits":false,"reason":"States that 60% of species are extinct, whereas the passage states that 60% of the world's ecosystem services are being degraded — not species.","error_type":"factual_error"},"B":{"fits":true,"reason":"Captures all key points — uniqueness, terminal uniqueness, the Millennium Ecosystem Assessment revealing overexploitation, and species facing extinction."},"C":{"fits":false,"reason":"States that 60% of species are extinct — the passage says 10-30% of species face extinction, and 60% refers to ecosystem services being degraded.","error_type":"factual_error"},"D":{"fits":false,"reason":"The Millennium Ecosystem Assessment does not say anywhere that we are unique — this misrepresents the source of the uniqueness claim.","error_type":"misrepresents_focus"}},"common_errors":{"A":"factual_error","C":"factual_error","D":"misrepresents_focus"},"difficulty":"medium","explanation":"The key points in the paragraph are\\n1. We are unique in some ways.\\n2. Our very uniqueness has landed us in a mess.\\n3. Overexploitation of resources has led to extinction of some species.\\nChoice A: It is rejected because it mentions that 60% of our species are extinct, whereas the para merely states that 60 percent of our ecosystem is being degraded.\\nChoice B: Correct. All the key points are captured in Choice B – ‘We know we are unique … precisely this kind of unique behaviour … ecosystem resources … used unsustainably … species face extinction…’.\\nChoice C: This is not right because it states that 60% of our species are extinct, whereas the para mentions 30%.\\nChoice D: This is wrong because the Millennium Ecosystem Assessment does not say anywhere that we are unique.\\nChoice (B)","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"instructions":"Directions for questions 1 to 30: Four alternative summaries are given below each text. Choose the option that best captures the essence of the text."},{"id":"BE_E7_002","exercise":"exercise_7","type":"best_essence","passage":"As with other change management programs, an important part of managing the risk of a format change is to build consensus within the organization. Managers must outline to employees the financial and competitive situation of the newspaper as frankly as possible explaining and communicating the need for change and the potential for growth once it has taken effect. A failure to reach at least some level of understanding will jeopardize the format-change program. With the broad outlines made clear, managers must convince the various stakeholders that change is required in view of the challenges facing the newspaper – declining circulation and advertising revenues, for example. Each department should present its perspective on the proposed changes, relating its outlook to the work it performs and outlining the actions required to make the transition successful. Managers must prepare carefully and keep the tone positive and constructive, since these conversations can be difficult. Although they are sensitive, they are essential to securing the all-important internal buy-in.","options":{"A":"Before a change in format can take place the management must seek the co-operation of all its employees. The details of the need for change must be known to them, as well as its repercussions. The challenges such as fall in revenue must be revealed, so that they can prepare for the change. These are sensitive issues but must be tackled positively for change to be successful.","B":"Like other change management programs, the management must prepare its employees for a format change. A consensus within the organization helps to overcome financial problems and face problems like loss of revenue and fall in market share.","C":"A consensus within the organization is needed before a newspaper can change its format. Managers must explain the need for change and the potential for growth. Other stakeholders must also be convinced and various departments prepared for a smooth transition. As these are sensitive issues, managers must prepare well and keep the tone positive.","D":"Any change requires that the employees in the organization are informed and their co-operation sought. Managers must adopt a positive attitude to these sensitive issues and tackle the difficult conversation constructively."},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"A consensus within the organization is needed before a newspaper can change its format. Managers must explain the need for change and the potential for growth. Other stakeholders must also be convinced and various departments prepared for a smooth transition. As these are sensitive issues, managers must prepare well and keep the tone positive.","option_analysis":{"A":{"fits":false,"reason":"Does not mention format change in a newspaper specifically — omits the central context of the passage.","error_type":"incomplete"},"B":{"fits":false,"reason":"Does not mention format change in a newspaper — omits the central context and also omits the potential for growth.","error_type":"incomplete"},"C":{"fits":true,"reason":"Covers all salient features — format change, consensus, the newspaper, need for change, potential for growth, various stakeholders, departments, transition, prepare carefully, tone positive, sensitive."},"D":{"fits":false,"reason":"Vaguely mentions 'any change' instead of format change in a newspaper — too general and omits valid specific information.","error_type":"too_general"}},"common_errors":{"A":"incomplete","B":"incomplete","D":"too_general"},"difficulty":"medium","explanation":"The main points covered are:\\n1. Format change needs consensus within the organisation.\\n2. Managers must explain the need for change and the potential for growth.\\n3. The tone must be positive.\\nChoice A: It does not mention format change in a newspaper.\\nChoice B: It also does not mention format change in a newspaper.\\nChoice C: Correct. It covers all salient features of the para – ‘format change …build consensus … the newspaper … need for change … potential for growth … various stakeholders … department … transition … prepare carefully … tone positive … sensitive’.\\nChoice D: It vaguely mentions ‘Any change’ and omits valid information. Choice (C)","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"instructions":"Directions for questions 1 to 30: Four alternative summaries are given below each text. Choose the option that best captures the essence of the text."},{"id":"BE_E7_003","exercise":"exercise_7","type":"best_essence","passage":"If you have a belief that is based entirely upon faith, I cannot examine your reasons. You retreat behind the private wall of faith where I cannot reach you. If your faith tells you that your quickest path to heaven is to murder a novelist and burn his books. I cannot dissuade you any more than I could argue the Yorkshire Ripper out of his faith that Jesus had 'called' him to cut up women. I can only start arguing with you if you abandon faith and listen to reason based upon evidence. This is what scientists professionally do. Science is a communal enterprise in which truths are established by appealing not to authority or private conviction but to public evidence and shared logic.","options":{"A":"A person whose belief is based on faith commits heinous acts like murder because he is not open to reason. You cannot reach such a person. Science, on the other hand, is based on logic, reason and evidence that is accessible to all.","B":"You cannot argue with a person whose belief is based on faith, as he/she is not open to reason based on evidence. In science truths are established by appealing not to authority or conviction but evidence and logic.","C":"You can only argue with people who are open to reason and not blinded by faith. Your faith can lead you to commit heinous crimes as can be seen from the Yorkshire ripper.","D":"Only a person whose belief is based on faith commits murder and destroys things, you can't reason with such a person. Science uses logic and evidence to build up its authority."},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"You cannot argue with a person whose belief is based on faith, as he/she is not open to reason based on evidence. In science truths are established by appealing not to authority or conviction but evidence and logic.","option_analysis":{"A":{"fits":false,"reason":"States that faith will lead to heinous crimes — the passage uses murder as an example of what faith could lead to, not a categorical claim about all faith-based beliefs.","error_type":"misrepresents_focus"},"B":{"fits":true,"reason":"Captures both main points — you cannot argue with faith-based reasoning, and science establishes truth through evidence and logic."},"C":{"fits":false,"reason":"Does not mention science at all — omits one of the two main points of the passage.","error_type":"incomplete"},"D":{"fits":false,"reason":"States that only a person with faith commits crimes — this is not what the passage says; the murder example is illustrative, not a categorical claim about all faith believers.","error_type":"misrepresents_focus"}},"common_errors":{"A":"misrepresents_focus","C":"incomplete","D":"misrepresents_focus"},"difficulty":"medium","explanation":"The main points made here are:\\n1. You can’t argue with a person whose belief is based on faith and who does not listen to reason.\\n2. Science establishes truth by appealing to evidence and logic.\\nChoice A: It says faith will lead to heinous crimes, which is wrong.\\nChoice B: Correct. It captures all the main points comprehensively.\\nChoice C: It does not mention science at all.\\nChoice D: It says that only a person with faith commits crimes, which is not true. Choice (B)","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"instructions":"Directions for questions 1 to 30: Four alternative summaries are given below each text. Choose the option that best captures the essence of the text."},{"id":"BE_E7_004","exercise":"exercise_7","type":"best_essence","passage":"The art of claymation (clay animation) has been around for almost a century from the very beginning of motion picture. However, it did not come into worldwide prominence until a BBC children's series 'Wallace and Gromit' started to win several Oscar awards. Claymation had been rescued from the fringes of the animation world. With computer graphics playing a part in almost every major movie, the 'brick and mortar' feel of Claymation won it a large fan base. However, it was only six years ago that Claymation landed in India, so the work done here, despite winning plaudits for its quality, has some way to go before it can catch up. But while Claymation stars may not throw any tantrums, they lend themselves to rather slow work, as every single frame has to be shot individually.","options":{"A":"The winning of Oscar by 'Wallace and Gromit' brought Claymation into prominence though it has been around since the beginning of motion pictures. It is only six years old in India and has a long way to go before it achieves quality.","B":"Claymation has been around since the beginning of motion picture but the BBC children series brought it to prominence. Its concrete feel, as opposed to computer animation, has won it fans. It involves a lot of work as every frame has to be shot.","C":"Unlike animation, Claymation involves a lot of work, as every frame has to be shot. But its brick and mortar feel has won it many fans. When the BBC series on Claymation won an Oscar, it zoomed into prominence and has a long way to go in India.","D":"Though Claymation is almost a century old, it received prominence only after 'Wallace and Gromit' won Oscars. The appeal of Claymation lies in its brick and mortar feel. It is relatively new to India, involves a lot of work and has a long way to go."},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"Though Claymation is almost a century old, it received prominence only after 'Wallace and Gromit' won Oscars. The appeal of Claymation lies in its brick and mortar feel. It is relatively new to India, involves a lot of work and has a long way to go.","option_analysis":{"A":{"fits":false,"reason":"States India has a long way to go before it achieves quality — the passage says India has won plaudits for quality but has a long way to go before it can catch up overall.","error_type":"factual_error"},"B":{"fits":false,"reason":"Does not mention the Indian scenario — omits a key point.","error_type":"incomplete"},"C":{"fits":false,"reason":"Erroneously claims that the BBC series was 'on Claymation' — it was on 'Wallace and Gromit', a claymation series, not a programme about Claymation.","error_type":"factual_error"},"D":{"fits":true,"reason":"Captures all main points precisely — almost a century old, Oscar prominence, brick and mortar appeal, new to India, slow work, long way to go."}},"common_errors":{"A":"factual_error","B":"incomplete","C":"factual_error"},"difficulty":"medium","explanation":"The main points are:\\n1. Claymation has been around for a century.\\n2. The winning of Oscars brought it prominence.\\n3. Claymation has been in India for 6 years and has a long way to go.\\nChoice A: This is not true, refer to ‘despite winning plaudits for its quality’.\\nChoice B: While plausible, this choice does not mention the Indian scenario.\\nChoice C: This choice erroneously claims that the BBC series was on Claymation, it was on ‘Wallace and Gromit’.\\nChoice D: Correct. This choice captures all the main points precisely. Choice (D)","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"instructions":"Directions for questions 1 to 30: Four alternative summaries are given below each text. Choose the option that best captures the essence of the text."},{"id":"BE_E7_005","exercise":"exercise_7","type":"best_essence","passage":"Human liver has sensors, that work with blood contents besides sugars, and these have the ability to affect hunger cues as well. There appear to be sensors that can detect the presence of specific amino acids. Many of these sensors, while shown to exist, have not been isolated. How those signals reach the brain and contribute to the cause of hunger is an even deeper mystery. The liver informs the brain of the body's nutritional status in many ways and this information somehow contributes to the feelings of hunger. It is physically separated from the brain by about thirty-eight centimeters or fifteen inches in most people, an infinite distance when viewed from the level of the molecule. To interact with the brain, the liver is forced to hurl its information via a system of interacting neurons. A nerve cell literally fires up a signal in response to a perceived metabolic stimulus, creating a chain of excitement that eventually reaches the brain.","options":{"A":"The sensors of the liver work with the blood contents to detect sugar and specific amino acids. They inform the brain through the nerve cells and nervous system of the nutritional status which contributes to the feeling of hunger.","B":"It is a mystery how the liver which is separated from the brain by about fifteen inches communicates with it. It is presumed that signals of hunger are fired to the brain through the nerve cells.","C":"The sensors of the liver, though not isolated, have been shown to have the ability to detect sugar and amino acids in the blood. How they communicate this information to the brain is not known as it is separated from the brain by about fifteen inches.","D":"The liver communicates feelings of hunger to the brain though it is separated by about fifteen inches which is an infinite distance from the point of view of a molecule. It sends messages through nerve cells."},"correct_answer":"A","correct_answer_value":"The sensors of the liver work with the blood contents to detect sugar and specific amino acids. They inform the brain through the nerve cells and nervous system of the nutritional status which contributes to the feeling of hunger.","option_analysis":{"A":{"fits":true,"reason":"Captures all three key points — sensors detecting sugar and amino acids, informing the brain through nerve cells, and contributing to feelings of hunger."},"B":{"fits":false,"reason":"Omits the first key point about sensors detecting sugar and amino acids — starts from the mystery of communication.","error_type":"incomplete"},"C":{"fits":false,"reason":"Is not conclusive — states 'how they communicate is not known' whereas the passage does explain the mechanism via neurons.","error_type":"misrepresents_focus"},"D":{"fits":false,"reason":"Does not mention sensors — omits the first and most important point about the liver's detection capability.","error_type":"incomplete"}},"common_errors":{"B":"incomplete","C":"misrepresents_focus","D":"incomplete"},"difficulty":"medium","explanation":"The main points are:\\n1. The liver has sensors that can detect sugar and amino acids in the blood.\\n2. It sends signals to the brain through nerve cells.\\n3. The information contributes to the feeling of hunger.\\nChoice A: Correct. This captures all the three points – ‘sensors … sugars … specific amino acids … brain … nutritional status … feelings of hunger … nerve cell’..\\nChoice B: It omits the first point.\\nChoice C: It is not conclusive.\\nChoice D: It does not mention sensors. Choice (A)","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"instructions":"Directions for questions 1 to 30: Four alternative summaries are given below each text. Choose the option that best captures the essence of the text."},{"id":"BE_E7_006","exercise":"exercise_7","type":"best_essence","passage":"The Manas river, after which the tiger reserve and the park are named, is known as Dang-me-chu in Bhutan. After entering India at Mathanguri, it splits into two major channels - the Bispani-Hakuwa and the Manas/Beki. The bed of this beautiful river, covered with boulders, can be seen through the crystal clear water during the non-flood season. A study carried out by Gauhati University found as many as 106 species of fish in this river. Owing to heavy poaching for consumption, the number of large-sized fish is being depleted which in turn has adversely affected the predators dependent upon the river. However according to a recent survey, the fish population is recovering. The trail of footprints on the sands of the river bank at Mathanguri left by tigers, wild elephants, deer and wild buffaloes during their movement towards the river overnight, shows that the river system is the only source of water for all the major fauna. For tigers, elephants, deer of all species, bisons, leopards and other cats, this is the only source of water during severe drought.","options":{"A":"The Manas or Dang-mi-chu enters India from Bhutan at Mathanguri. It splits into two-Bispani and Manas. The boulder covered bed can be seen through its crystal clear water which houses 106 varieties of fishes.","B":"The Manas river after entering India at Mathanguri splits into two. It has crystal clear water and is the main source for the wildlife here as seen from the footprints of animals that come here at night.","C":"The Manas or Dang-me-chu as it is known in Bhutan splits into two - Bispani and Manas - on entering India at Mathanguri. It has crystal clear water, a boulder covered bed and 106 varieties of fish which are being lost through poaching. It is also the main source of water for the wildlife around, which is proved by the footprints of the animals near the river.","D":"The Manas river splits into two after entering India at Mathanguri. It has a variety of fish, which are getting depleted because of poaching. It is the source of water for the wildlife as seen from the footprints of animals."},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"The Manas river splits into two after entering India at Mathanguri. It has a variety of fish, which are getting depleted because of poaching. It is the source of water for the wildlife as seen from the footprints of animals.","option_analysis":{"A":{"fits":false,"reason":"Does not include the crucial third point — the river as the only source of water for fauna.","error_type":"incomplete"},"B":{"fits":false,"reason":"Does not mention the species of fish or the poaching problem — omits the second key point.","error_type":"incomplete"},"C":{"fits":false,"reason":"Does justice to the passage but includes too many details making it more of a description than a summary.","error_type":"too_narrow"},"D":{"fits":true,"reason":"Precisely covers all three key points — river entering India and splitting, fish being depleted through poaching, and source of water for wildlife."}},"common_errors":{"A":"incomplete","B":"incomplete","C":"too_narrow"},"difficulty":"easy","explanation":"The main points are:\\n1. The Manas river enters India at Mathanguri and splits into two.\\n2. It has a variety of fish depleted through poaching.\\n3. It is the only source of water for the fauna.\\nChoice A: It does not include the crucial third point.\\nChoice B: It does not mention the species of fish.\\nChoice C: It does justice to the para but includes too many details.\\nChoice D: Correct. It precisely covers all the three points mentioned above. Choice (D)","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"instructions":"Directions for questions 1 to 30: Four alternative summaries are given below each text. Choose the option that best captures the essence of the text."},{"id":"BE_E7_007","exercise":"exercise_7","type":"best_essence","passage":"Not too long ago, anyone suggesting a link between spirituality and good health would have been laughed out of the room. But thanks to efforts of doctors like Deepak Chopra, who popularized the concept of holistic healing, spirituality is no longer taboo in the world of science and medicine. A preliminary study by University of Chicago researchers contends that belief in God may improve a person's physical health. The study says strong spirituality, regardless of religion, results in improved physiological functioning, health and well-being. The University has received $1.8 million to conduct a comprehensive study to examine the connection between religious belief and health. The fact that a major research University is exploring the link between religion and health is a big step in recognizing the importance of spirituality in our lives.","options":{"A":"Spirituality is not a taboo to science and medical research shows that belief in God, irrespective of religion, results in better health. The growing importance of spirituality is borne out by the research work being carried out in major universities.","B":"It is not longer unfashionable to suggest a link between spirituality and health, ever since Deepak Chopra popularized the concept of holistic healing. Universities are prepared to conduct research to establish a link between religion, any religion for that matter, and good health, thereby accepting the importance of spirituality.","C":"Ever since Deepak Chopra popularized the concept of holistic healing, universities have been researching into the link between religion and health though it has been established that a belief in God leads to better health.","D":"Pray to God for good health seems to be the latest trend since Deepak Chopra popularized the concept of holistic healing. Spirituality has come into its own as universities conduct research to establish a link between religious belief and good health."},"correct_answer":"A","correct_answer_value":"Spirituality is not a taboo to science and medical research shows that belief in God, irrespective of religion, results in better health. The growing importance of spirituality is borne out by the research work being carried out in major universities.","option_analysis":{"A":{"fits":true,"reason":"Links spirituality to science, health and research aptly — covers all three main points."},"B":{"fits":false,"reason":"States universities are 'prepared' to conduct research — the passage says they are already doing it; also misses point 2 about belief in God improving health.","error_type":"factual_error"},"C":{"fits":false,"reason":"States the link between belief in God and good health 'has been established' — the passage says 'may improve', making this a distortion.","error_type":"contradicts_passage"},"D":{"fits":false,"reason":"Mentions 'the latest trend' which is not supported by the passage — the passage treats this as a serious scientific development, not a trend.","error_type":"out_of_scope"}},"common_errors":{"B":"factual_error","C":"contradicts_passage","D":"out_of_scope"},"difficulty":"medium","explanation":"The main points made are:\\n1. Spirituality is no longer a taboo in the world of science and medicine.\\n2. A preliminary study says belief in God may improve a person’s health\\n3. The involvement of a major university shows the importance of spirituality.\\nChoice A: Correct. This choice links spirituality to science, health and research aptly.\\nChoice B: It is too long, misses out point 2 and states erroneously that universities are ‘prepared’ whereas they are already into it.\\nChoice C: It is wrong in saying that the link between belief in God and good health has been established, as the para says ‘may improve’.\\nChoice D: It mentions ‘the latest trend’ which is not true. Choice (A)","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"instructions":"Directions for questions 1 to 30: Four alternative summaries are given below each text. Choose the option that best captures the essence of the text."},{"id":"BE_E7_008","exercise":"exercise_7","type":"best_essence","passage":"How much should the government spend on education? According to the National Policy of Education, it is 6 percent of the GDP. India spends a little less than 4 percent, as did countries like South Korea and Japan that achieved high literacy post World War II. China spends only 2.6 percent. The quality of expenditure is more important than the quantity. The center spends ₹1,000 per year on a child while in cities like Mumbai, it's higher than ₹4,400. Yet, independent studies have found that an average four years of schooling generates two years of learning levels across country. Most government spending is like water carried in a bucket with a hole – in primary education, salaries account for 97 percent of the budget. But the proponents of higher spending-for-more-education pin their hopes on some part of the increased spending finally reaching the beneficiary.","options":{"A":"Where education is concerned, how well money is spent is more important than how much is spent, as seen from the progress made by some Asian countries. In India, much of the expenditure on education seems to be wasted without benefiting the intended beneficiary.","B":"What percentage of GDP should be spent on education is open to question since some Asian countries that spend less or as much as India have achieved greater progress. Expenditure on education is like water leaking from a bucket – it is wasted.","C":"India spends 4% of its GDP on education as against the 6% considered ideal by the National Education Policy. However, various Asian countries spending less have achieved greater progress than India, which makes us realise that much of the expenditure is wasted in India.","D":"Though the government spends a sizeable amount per child on education, much of it seems to be going on overheads like salaries etc. As such, India has not progressed as much as other Asian countries."},"correct_answer":"A","correct_answer_value":"Where education is concerned, how well money is spent is more important than how much is spent, as seen from the progress made by some Asian countries. In India, much of the expenditure on education seems to be wasted without benefiting the intended beneficiary.","option_analysis":{"A":{"fits":true,"reason":"Covers both main points — quality over quantity of expenditure, and spending not reaching the intended beneficiaries."},"B":{"fits":false,"reason":"Does not mention the quality aspect — focuses only on wastage without the key quality vs quantity distinction.","error_type":"incomplete"},"C":{"fits":false,"reason":"Does not mention what happened to the beneficiary — omits the second key point about spending not reaching beneficiaries.","error_type":"incomplete"},"D":{"fits":false,"reason":"Does not mention the first point about quality being more important than quantity.","error_type":"incomplete"}},"common_errors":{"B":"incomplete","C":"incomplete","D":"incomplete"},"difficulty":"medium","explanation":"The main points are:\\n1. Expenditure on education – quality is more important than quantity.\\n2. Spending not reaching the intended beneficiaries.\\nChoice A: Correct. It covers the main points – ‘high literacy post … quality of expenditure … water carried in a bucket with a hole...’.\\nChoice B: It does not mention the quality aspect, only wastage.\\nChoice C: It does not mention what happened to the beneficiary.\\nChoice D: It does not mention the first point. Choice (A)","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"instructions":"Directions for questions 1 to 30: Four alternative summaries are given below each text. Choose the option that best captures the essence of the text."},{"id":"BE_E7_009","exercise":"exercise_7","type":"best_essence","passage":"Nuclear energy has a serious reputation problem, due to public memory of past accidents and crises that span generations. For example, Ukraine's Chernobyl explosion (1986) and Japan's Fukushima accident (2011) resulted in extensive environmental destruction that lasted for years. Many environmental groups are actively protesting nuclear energy and the way it is being presented as 'clean energy.' Since it was first commercialized, an activist says, [nuclear power] has proven to be one of the dirtiest, most dangerous and most expensive sources of energy. Nuclear reactors have a long history of accidents, leaks, extended outages and skyrocketing costs.","options":{"A":"Nuclear energy has been proved to be deleterious and thus, should not be entertained.","B":"The popular outcry against nuclear energy is likely to make it not viable.","C":"In the absence of nuclear energy, the world is likely to face a serious energy crisis.","D":"There are serious apprehensions about nuclear power as a potential source of energy."},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"There are serious apprehensions about nuclear power as a potential source of energy.","option_analysis":{"A":{"fits":false,"reason":"The passage presents concerns about nuclear energy but does not assert that it is definitively harmful or should be avoided altogether.","error_type":"misrepresents_focus"},"B":{"fits":false,"reason":"The passage does not make a definitive statement about the future viability of nuclear energy.","error_type":"out_of_scope"},"C":{"fits":false,"reason":"The passage focuses on concerns about nuclear energy but does not suggest the world would face an energy crisis without it.","error_type":"out_of_scope"},"D":{"fits":true,"reason":"Captures the essence without overstating — reflects the concerns and apprehensions expressed without making a definitive judgment."}},"common_errors":{"A":"misrepresents_focus","B":"out_of_scope","C":"out_of_scope"},"difficulty":"easy","explanation":"The passage discusses the public’s negative perception of nuclear energy, focusing on past accidents like Chernobyl and Fukushima, and highlighting the concerns raised by environmental groups regarding the dangers and costs associated with nuclear power. The passage does not claim that nuclear energy is inherently harmful or should not be used, nor does it make a definitive statement about the world’s energy future without it.\\nOption A: incorrect because while the passage presents concerns, it does not assert that nuclear energy is definitively harmful or that it should be avoided altogether.\\nOption B: incorrect because the passage does suggest a public outcry, but it does not make a definitive statement about the viability of nuclear energy in the future.\\nOption C: incorrect because the passage focuses on concerns about nuclear energy, but it does not suggest that the world would face an energy crisis without it.\\nOption D; correct because this captures the essence of the passage. It reflects the concerns and apprehensions expressed in the passage without overstating them.\\nThus, the correct option is Choice (D)","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"instructions":"Directions for questions 1 to 30: Four alternative summaries are given below each text. Choose the option that best captures the essence of the text."},{"id":"BE_E7_010","exercise":"exercise_7","type":"best_essence","passage":"For those sympathetic to Aesthetic Protectionism, the idea of 'positive aesthetics' has seemed to offer an attractive solution to some of its difficulties. This is the idea that every part of nature, properly seen, is beautiful (or alternatively, that there is no ugliness in nature). 'Properly seen' here has often been understood to mean 'seen in light of scientific understanding'. If correct, the implication of positive aesthetics would seem to be that no environment or species is, in principle, beyond the pale of aesthetic protection.","options":{"A":"Aesthetic appreciation has at its base an objective and scientific understanding.","B":"Sympathisers of aesthetic protectionism rely on scientific understanding to justify the universal beauty of nature.","C":"Aesthetic Protectionism is just one of the many reasons for environmental protection.","D":"Nature deserves to be protected even for its aesthetic value."},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"Nature deserves to be protected even for its aesthetic value.","option_analysis":{"A":{"fits":false,"reason":"While the passage connects aesthetic appreciation to scientific understanding, the primary point is the implication of positive aesthetics for protecting nature — not the basis of aesthetic appreciation.","error_type":"too_narrow"},"B":{"fits":false,"reason":"The passage does not discuss the ideals of sympathisers of aesthetic protection — this focuses on a secondary character rather than the main implication.","error_type":"misrepresents_focus"},"C":{"fits":false,"reason":"The passage does not position Aesthetic Protectionism as one among many reasons for environmental protection — this introduces a comparison not in the passage.","error_type":"out_of_scope"},"D":{"fits":true,"reason":"Captures the core idea — the implication of positive aesthetics is that all of nature has aesthetic worth and deserves protection."}},"common_errors":{"A":"too_narrow","B":"misrepresents_focus","C":"out_of_scope"},"difficulty":"medium","explanation":"The passage discusses the concept of “positive aesthetics,” which asserts that all of nature, when properly understood through a scientific lens, is inherently beautiful. This belief supports the idea that all environments and species have intrinsic aesthetic value, making them deserving of protection. We need to identify the option that reflects this essence.\\nOption A: incorrect because while the passage connects aesthetic appreciation to scientific understanding (“properly seen”), the primary point is the implication of positive aesthetics for protecting nature.\\nOption B: incorrect because the passage does not discuss the ideal of sympathisers of aesthetic protection.\\nOption C: incorrect because the passage does not discuss other reasons for environmental protection or position aesthetic protectionism as one among many reasons. This makes the option irrelevant.\\nOption D: correct because it captures the core idea of the passage, which is the implication of positive aesthetics in ensuring that all of nature has aesthetic worth and deserves protection.\\nThus, the correct option is Choice (D)","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"instructions":"Directions for questions 1 to 30: Four alternative summaries are given below each text. Choose the option that best captures the essence of the text."},{"id":"BE_E7_011","exercise":"exercise_7","type":"best_essence","passage":"Human thought is marked by its capacity to envision possibilities beyond the immediate and the tangible. This ability spans diverse domains, from creating fictional worlds to simulating potential outcomes of future events. By bridging memory and foresight, it enables individuals to draw lessons from past experiences and anticipate a variety of scenarios. Such a faculty is foundational not only to artistic creativity but also to solving real-world problems and making informed decisions, demonstrating its crucial role in both abstract and practical aspects of human cognition.","options":{"A":"People use imagination to integrate past experiences and plan for future possibilities.","B":"Imagination helps individuals make sense of abstract and unfamiliar concepts, aiding their understanding.","C":"Imagination is an essential cognitive ability, serving many purposes to benefit us.","D":"Practical challenges in life are often addressed using creative and cognitive tools, including imagination."},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"Imagination is an essential cognitive ability, serving many purposes to benefit us.","option_analysis":{"A":{"fits":false,"reason":"Reflects the passage's mention of imagination bridging memory and foresight but focuses narrowly on just one aspect rather than the broader multifaceted applications.","error_type":"too_narrow"},"B":{"fits":false,"reason":"Captures imagination's role in understanding abstract concepts but doesn't address imagination's contributions to practical problem-solving — incomplete.","error_type":"incomplete"},"C":{"fits":true,"reason":"Accurately captures the main idea by emphasizing the multifaceted role of imagination in both abstract and practical domains."},"D":{"fits":false,"reason":"Diminishes the focus on imagination's central role by grouping it with other cognitive tools — misrepresents its primacy.","error_type":"misrepresents_focus"}},"common_errors":{"A":"too_narrow","B":"incomplete","D":"misrepresents_focus"},"difficulty":"medium","explanation":"The passage emphasizes the unique human ability to think beyond the immediate and the tangible. This ability, which connects memory and foresight, is foundational to creative endeavours and practical problem-solving. It allows individuals to draw lessons from the past, anticipate future scenarios, and address both abstract and real-world challenges, underscoring its multifaceted role in human cognition.\\nOption A: incorrect because while it reflects the passage’s mention of imagination bridging memory and foresight, it focuses narrowly on just one aspect of imagination (linking past and future) rather than its broader applications, making it less comprehensive than the correct option.\\nOption B: incorrect because while this option captures imagination’s role in understanding abstract concepts, which is implied in the passage, it doesn’t address imagination’s contributions to practical problem-solving or other cognitive benefits, making it incomplete.\\nOption C: correct because this option accurately captures the main idea of the passage by emphasizing the multifaceted role of imagination in both abstract and practical domains.\\nOption D: incorrect because while imagination is relevant to solving practical problems, this option diminishes the focus on imagination’s central role by grouping it with other cognitive tools.\\nThus, the correct option is Choice (C)","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"instructions":"Directions for questions 1 to 30: Four alternative summaries are given below each text. Choose the option that best captures the essence of the text."},{"id":"BE_E7_012","exercise":"exercise_7","type":"best_essence","passage":"Unseasonal cyclones and storms are becoming more frequent and intense, influenced by changing climate patterns. These events cause widespread damage to infrastructure, ecosystems, and livelihoods, with their impacts disproportionately affecting vulnerable populations, such as those in coastal areas, low-income groups, and people without adequate access to disaster preparedness resources. Despite these risks, public awareness about the unpredictability and severity of such weather events remains low. Studies in cyclone-prone regions suggest that people often underestimate their vulnerability. To address this gap, it is crucial to focus on how unseasonal cyclones are framed in public discourse. The media can play a pivotal role in educating people about the dangers of these storms and the broader societal and infrastructural disruptions they may cause, fostering a culture of preparedness.","options":{"A":"Public awareness about the increasing destruction caused by unseasonal storms and cyclones needs to be enhanced by effectively framing their risks and impacts through media coverage, making people ready for contingencies.","B":"The increasing frequency of unseasonal cyclones and storms suggests that early-warning systems are functioning effectively, assisting the role already being played by the media.","C":"The media should focus more on reporting the benefits of cyclone preparedness programs than on the societal and infrastructural damages caused by storms and cyclones.","D":"Public awareness efforts are best directed towards minimizing government interventions in cyclone-prone regions, as these populations can better cope without external support."},"correct_answer":"A","correct_answer_value":"Public awareness about the increasing destruction caused by unseasonal storms and cyclones needs to be enhanced by effectively framing their risks and impacts through media coverage, making people ready for contingencies.","option_analysis":{"A":{"fits":true,"reason":"Aligns with the passage's main idea — the need for public awareness and the media's role in educating people and fostering a culture of preparedness."},"B":{"fits":false,"reason":"States early-warning systems are functioning effectively — the passage highlights that people underestimate their vulnerability, suggesting systems are not effective enough.","error_type":"contradicts_passage"},"C":{"fits":false,"reason":"Misrepresents the passage — the passage emphasises educating people on risks and societal impacts, not on reporting benefits of preparedness programs.","error_type":"misrepresents_focus"},"D":{"fits":false,"reason":"Contrary to the passage — the passage focuses on the vulnerability of people in cyclone-prone regions and the need for better preparedness and support, not minimizing government intervention.","error_type":"contradicts_passage"}},"common_errors":{"B":"contradicts_passage","C":"misrepresents_focus","D":"contradicts_passage"},"difficulty":"medium","explanation":"The passage discusses the impact of increasing frequency of cyclones and storms, focusing on the need for preparedness and on the role of media in creating this awareness.\\nOption A: correct because it aligns with the passage’s main idea, which emphasizes the need for public awareness regarding the severity and unpredictability of unseasonal cyclones and storms. The passage advocates for focusing on how these events are framed in public discourse and stresses the importance of the media in educating people, thus making them more prepared for such contingencies.\\nOption B: incorrect because the passage highlights that early-warning systems are not performing as expected due to the underestimation of risk by vulnerable populations.\\nOption C: incorrect because this misrepresents the passage. The passage emphasizes educating the public on the risks and societal impacts of storms, rather than focusing on the benefits of preparedness programs. It suggests that the media’s role is to highlight the dangers and disruptions caused by cyclones, not just the benefits of preparedness.\\nOption D: incorrect because this is contrary to the passage’s content. The passage focuses on the vulnerability of people in cyclone-prone regions and suggests the need for better preparedness and government support. It does not advocate for minimizing interventions, which would be detrimental given the context of vulnerability and the need for assistance.\\nThus, the correct option is Choice (A)","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"instructions":"Directions for questions 1 to 30: Four alternative summaries are given below each text. Choose the option that best captures the essence of the text."},{"id":"BE_E7_013","exercise":"exercise_7","type":"best_essence","passage":"The art-artist debate presents two conflicting views. Some argue that art can be separated from the artist, meaning that while an artist may be a 'bad guy,' their art can still hold value. In this view, it's the artist's actions that should be condemned, not the art itself. Others believe the artist is dead once the art is created, meaning viewers reinterpret it in their own way, free from the artist's influence. They argue that emotions evoked by art no longer tie back to the artist. Hence, appreciating or supporting art doesn't equate to endorsing the artist's actions.","options":{"A":"An artwork always lives up to the expectations of viewers and anticipation of the creator.","B":"An artwork's worth is surely influenced by the character of its creator.","C":"An artist's personal behaviour has bearing on the worth of his artwork.","D":"Whether art-artist dichotomy holds good depends on individual opinion."},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"Whether art-artist dichotomy holds good depends on individual opinion.","option_analysis":{"A":{"fits":false,"reason":"Assumes that art always meets expectations — contradicts the passage's discussion of subjective and conflicting views on art appreciation.","error_type":"contradicts_passage"},"B":{"fits":false,"reason":"While some may believe this, the passage presents conflicting views — one side argues art can be entirely separated from the artist.","error_type":"too_narrow"},"C":{"fits":false,"reason":"Similar to B — presents only one side of the debate as the conclusion, ignoring the other view that art is independent of the artist.","error_type":"too_narrow"},"D":{"fits":true,"reason":"Most accurate — the passage presents two conflicting views, showing that the art-artist debate is subjective and depends on individual perspective."}},"common_errors":{"A":"contradicts_passage","B":"too_narrow","C":"too_narrow"},"difficulty":"medium","explanation":"The passage discusses two opposing views on the relationship between an artist and their artwork. One side argues that the artist’s personal behaviour should not affect the value of their art, while the other suggests that emotions and interpretations of art are independent of the artist’s personal life. The key issue is whether the artist’s actions should influence the evaluation of their work.\\nOption A: incorrect because this statement assumes that art always meets expectations, which contradicts the passage’s discussion. It overlooks the subjective nature of art appreciation and the reinterpretation of art by viewers.\\nOption B: incorrect because while some may believe that an artist’s behaviour influences the worth of their art, the passage suggests that others argue the art can be separated from the artist.\\nOption C: incorrect because while some may hold this belief, the passage discusses conflicting views, with one side arguing that an artist’s behaviour does not influence the value of the art.\\nOption D: correct because this is the most accurate statement. The passage presents two conflicting views, showing that the art-artist debate is subjective and based on individual perspective.\\nThus, the correct option is Choice (D)","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"instructions":"Directions for questions 1 to 30: Four alternative summaries are given below each text. Choose the option that best captures the essence of the text."},{"id":"BE_E7_014","exercise":"exercise_7","type":"best_essence","passage":"If de-globalisation is a myth and if the globalisation of trade and capital flows continues apace, then who are the winners and losers? The winners obviously are Emerging Markets. Large global companies listed in the Western world are also major beneficiaries of the continued rise of Emerging Markets. The casualties of globalisation are inefficient companies – regardless of location – whose factories and operations continue to be predominantly domestic or whose domestic factories have been unable to rise to the challenge of introducing labour-saving technology such as robotization. Unless sheltered from global competition by tariffs or specific local factors (e.g. the market for barbers is necessarily local, not global), they will be unable to compete with efficient companies operating on a global scale without necessarily being giants.","options":{"A":"Globalization necessitates the survival of the efficient, despite their size.","B":"Globalization favours multi-national companies which are efficient despite their size.","C":"Globalization penalizes inefficient companies by removing them from the markets.","D":"Local incentives act against the spirit of globalization that favours efficient companies."},"correct_answer":"A","correct_answer_value":"Globalization necessitates the survival of the efficient, despite their size.","option_analysis":{"A":{"fits":true,"reason":"Accurately captures the essence — efficiency, not size, determines success in a globalized market; both large multinationals and small efficient firms can win."},"B":{"fits":false,"reason":"Overemphasizes multinationals — the passage also mentions small efficient companies operating globally can thrive, making this too narrow.","error_type":"too_narrow"},"C":{"fits":false,"reason":"The passage doesn't say inefficient companies are explicitly removed from markets — some may survive due to local protectionism or tariffs.","error_type":"misrepresents_focus"},"D":{"fits":false,"reason":"The passage does not suggest local factors act against the spirit of globalization — they are simply presented as exceptions.","error_type":"misrepresents_focus"}},"common_errors":{"B":"too_narrow","C":"misrepresents_focus","D":"misrepresents_focus"},"difficulty":"hard","explanation":"The passage argues that globalization continues to benefit Emerging Markets and efficient multinational companies, irrespective of size. Conversely, inefficient companies that rely on domestic operations and fail to adopt modern labour-saving technologies (like robotization) struggle to survive unless they are protected by tariffs or operate in inherently local markets.\\nOption A: correct because this accurately captures the essence of the passage. It highlights that efficiency, not size, determines success in a globalized market. Both large multinational companies and smaller, efficient firms operating globally are winners, while inefficiency is the key determinant of failure.\\nOption B: incorrect because while it is true that globalization benefits multinational companies, this option overemphasizes the role of multinationals. The passage also mentions that small efficient companies operating globally can thrive, making this option too narrow to be accurate.\\nOption C: incorrect because while globalization does challenge inefficient companies, the passage doesn’t explicitly state that they are “removed from the markets.” Some may survive due to local protectionism, tariffs, or operating in local markets (like barbers).\\nOption D: incorrect because while the passage mentions local factors (e.g., the barber industry) and tariffs, it doesn’t suggest they act “against the spirit of globalization.”\\nThus, the correct option is Choice (A)","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"instructions":"Directions for questions 1 to 30: Four alternative summaries are given below each text. Choose the option that best captures the essence of the text."},{"id":"BE_E7_015","exercise":"exercise_7","type":"best_essence","passage":"Planetarity-as-intimacy is a novel concept that is very important for us. In our analysis, the planet is no longer the mute background of people's lives, or merely the hyper-object that is always too big and too remote to even be felt by them. Instead, the natural history of the planet is extremely accelerated today, it now unfolds roughly at the scale of a human life: the change of climate, the loss of biodiversity, but also the growth of the techno-sphere and the emergence of new biological and physical phenomena like genetic engineering and particle accelerators are not only experienced by people as they move through their life stages, it also affects how their lives unfold. It is intimately linked to the shape of their biographies by creating new work opportunities, rendering them unemployed, forcing them to migrate away from climate-related conflicts, or shortening their lives through heat-related causes.","options":{"A":"Planetarity-as-intimacy brings out the inseparable relation between humans and the planet, the relation being symbiotic.","B":"Planetarity-as-intimacy criticizes the role humans play, resulting in detrimental impact on the planet.","C":"Planetarity-as-intimacy brings out the rapid pace at which human-planetary relations are evolving.","D":"Planetarity-as-intimacy emphasizes the intimate and reciprocal relationship between humans and the planet."},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"Planetarity-as-intimacy emphasizes the intimate and reciprocal relationship between humans and the planet.","option_analysis":{"A":{"fits":false,"reason":"'Symbiotic' overstates the relationship — the passage describes planetary changes affecting human lives, not mutual biological benefit.","error_type":"misrepresents_focus"},"B":{"fits":false,"reason":"The passage does not criticize humans — it describes how planetary changes intimately affect human lives, not blame humans for the planet's condition.","error_type":"misrepresents_focus"},"C":{"fits":false,"reason":"While rapid evolution is mentioned, this doesn't capture the central theme of reciprocal, intimate connection between humans and the planet.","error_type":"too_narrow"},"D":{"fits":true,"reason":"Accurately captures the essence — the relationship is intimate and reciprocal, planetary changes directly shaping human biographies."}},"common_errors":{"A":"misrepresents_focus","B":"misrepresents_focus","C":"too_narrow"},"difficulty":"hard","explanation":"The passage introduces the concept of “Planetarity-as-intimacy,” which suggests that the relationship between humans and the planet has evolved from being a distant, passive backdrop to being actively experienced and intertwined with human lives. The changes in the planet, such as climate change, biodiversity loss, and the growth of technology, now unfold on a timescale similar to that of human lives. These planetary changes directly impact people’s lives, shaping their work opportunities, causing unemployment, forcing migration due to climate-related conflicts, and even shortening lives due to extreme weather.\\nOption A: incorrect because the term ‘symbiotic’ overstates the relationship, implying mutual benefit in a biological sense, which is not the primary focus of the passage.\\nOption B: incorrect because while the passage discusses how human lives are shaped by planetary changes, it does not criticize humans directly nor does it focus solely on the detrimental impacts. The emphasis is more on how planetary changes intimately affect human lives, rather than on blaming humans for the planet’s condition.\\nOption C: incorrect because while the rapid evolution of the relationship is mentioned, it doesn’t capture the central theme of the reciprocal, intimate connection between humans and the planet.\\nOption D: correct because this accurately captures the essence of the passage. The relationship is described as one that is intimately tied to human lives and unfolds through the direct impact of planetary changes. The word “reciprocal” is key, as it reflects the passage’s idea that these changes not only affect humans but also shape their lives.\\nThus, the correct option is Choice (D)","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"instructions":"Directions for questions 1 to 30: Four alternative summaries are given below each text. Choose the option that best captures the essence of the text."},{"id":"BE_E7_016","exercise":"exercise_7","type":"best_essence","passage":"A conspicuous aspect of our shared human experience – and of its inherent diversity – arises from our understanding of ourselves as autonomous individuals, beings capable of defining and ordering our lives based on subjective conceptions of what is good for us. This ability to make choices and determine the direction of our own lives brings to the forefront, for all of us, the fundamental question of what constitutes justice and ideal behaviour, both of which form the central norms not only for human individuals but also for the society.","options":{"A":"The individual perception of justice and societal norms serves as both an integrating factor within society and a differentiating factor between individuals.","B":"Individuals with the commonality of shared experiences remain individuals, despite being a part of a larger collection of society.","C":"Subjective conceptions of justice and collective acceptance of its standards form the intricate fibre connecting individuals into society.","D":"Justice and ideal behaviour are complex ideas in a society, given the individualistic perspective of constituent members of the society."},"correct_answer":"A","correct_answer_value":"The individual perception of justice and societal norms serves as both an integrating factor within society and a differentiating factor between individuals.","option_analysis":{"A":{"fits":true,"reason":"Aligns well with the passage's idea that individual perceptions of justice connect people to society while also creating distinctions between individuals."},"B":{"fits":false,"reason":"Touches on individual autonomy but doesn't address the link to societal norms or the question of justice and ideal behaviour.","error_type":"incomplete"},"C":{"fits":false,"reason":"The passage does not suggest that subjective conceptions of justice and collective acceptance form an 'intricate fibre' connecting individuals — this overstates the connection.","error_type":"misrepresents_focus"},"D":{"fits":false,"reason":"While it mentions the complexity of justice, it does not address the core interaction between individual and society regarding justice and norms.","error_type":"incomplete"}},"common_errors":{"B":"incomplete","C":"misrepresents_focus","D":"incomplete"},"difficulty":"hard","explanation":"The passage discusses the shared human experience and how autonomy and subjective conceptions of justice shape both the individual and societal norms. It emphasizes that our ability to define and order our lives leads to the complex question of justice and ideal behaviour, which ultimately ties individuals to societal structures.\\nOption A: correct because it aligns well with the passage’s idea that individual perceptions of justice connect people to society, while also creating distinctions.\\nOption B: incorrect because this option touches on the concept of individual autonomy but doesn’t fully capture the link to societal norms or the question of justice. While it is true that individuals remain unique within society, the idea of justice or ideal behaviour is not addressed here, making this option less comprehensive.\\nOption C: incorrect because the passage does not suggest that subjective conceptions of justice and collective acceptance of its standards ‘form the intricate fibre’ connecting individuals to society.\\nOption D: incorrect because while it mentions the complexity of justice, it does not address the core interaction between individual and society regarding justice and norms.\\nThus, the correct option is Choice (A)","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"instructions":"Directions for questions 1 to 30: Four alternative summaries are given below each text. Choose the option that best captures the essence of the text."},{"id":"BE_E7_017","exercise":"exercise_7","type":"best_essence","passage":"History can provide strong insight into flawed premises. The most well-documented cases are of racial and gender biases malforming all-too-many studies of human difference, some with tragic consequences, as in the case of eugenics researchers who recommended policies of social exclusion, sterilisation and even genocide. In January 2023, the world's largest body of human geneticists, the American Society of Human Genetics, apologised for the role of its past members in promoting racism, eugenics and other forms of discrimination. The board's pledge to do better in the future was grounded in historical knowledge of its past. This time of reckoning with history is overdue, 'but it forms the foundation for a brighter future.","options":{"A":"The history of science is dotted with experiences not worthy of science.","B":"The basic premises underlying science are questionable and not logically sound.","C":"History of science warns us of the dangers of misuse of science.","D":"Understanding history is vital for addressing past scientific errors and building a better future."},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"History of science warns us of the dangers of misuse of science.","option_analysis":{"A":{"fits":false,"reason":"Implies a broad critique of science itself — the passage focuses on misuse of science in discriminatory contexts, not broadly condemning scientific experiences.","error_type":"misrepresents_focus"},"B":{"fits":false,"reason":"Does not argue against the premises of science itself — the passage criticises how biases have corrupted the application of science, not science's foundations.","error_type":"misrepresents_focus"},"C":{"fits":true,"reason":"Explicitly captures how the misuse of scientific ideas like eugenics has had harmful consequences, and warns against repeating such mistakes."},"D":{"fits":false,"reason":"While valid in a general sense, the passage does not suggest that understanding history is 'vital' — it describes a specific instance of reckoning, not a universal principle.","error_type":"too_general"}},"common_errors":{"A":"misrepresents_focus","B":"misrepresents_focus","D":"too_general"},"difficulty":"medium","explanation":"The passage discusses how history reveals flawed premises in science, such as racial and gender biases, leading to harmful practices like eugenics. It highlights an apology from the American Society of Human Genetics for its past promotion of discriminatory ideas, emphasizing that acknowledging historical mistakes is crucial for building a better future.\\nOption A: Incorrect. While it implies a critique of past practices, the passage focuses on the misuse of science, particularly in discriminatory contexts, rather than broadly condemning experiences in science as unworthy.\\nOption B: Incorrect. The passage does not argue against the premises of science itself but criticizes how biases have historically corrupted its application.\\nOption C: Correct. The passage explicitly describes how the misuse of scientific ideas, such as eugenics, has had harmful consequences, demonstrating the importance of learning from history to avoid repeating such mistakes.\\nOption D: incorrect because while valid in a general sense, this passage does not suggest about the ‘vital’ nature of this understanding\\nThus, the correct option is Choice (C)","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"instructions":"Directions for questions 1 to 30: Four alternative summaries are given below each text. Choose the option that best captures the essence of the text."},{"id":"BE_E7_018","exercise":"exercise_7","type":"best_essence","passage":"Stoicism, which is considered – when considered at all – a philosophy of grim endurance, of carrying on rather than getting over, of tolerating rather than transcending life's agonies and adversities. No wonder it's not more popular. No wonder the Stoic sage, in Western culture, has never obtained the popularity of the Zen master. Even though Stoicism is far more accessible, not only does it lack the exotic mystique of Eastern practice; it's also regarded as a philosophy of merely breaking even while remaining determinedly impassive. What this attitude ignores is the promise proffered by Stoicism of lasting transcendence and imperturbable tranquillity.","options":{"A":"Stoicism seldom gets the attention it deserves.","B":"The popular conception of Stoicism is ill-informed.","C":"The popularity of Stoicism is generally due to the misplaced emphasis.","D":"Unlike other philosophies, Stoicism offers peace through endurance."},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"The popular conception of Stoicism is ill-informed.","option_analysis":{"A":{"fits":false,"reason":"While the passage mentions Stoicism is not more popular, it focuses on why it is misunderstood rather than the lack of attention it receives — too general.","error_type":"too_general"},"B":{"fits":true,"reason":"Correctly identifies the core argument — the passage critiques how Stoicism is perceived as merely about 'endurance' rather than offering deeper promises of peace and transcendence."},"C":{"fits":false,"reason":"The passage states Stoicism is 'not more popular' and attributes this to being mischaracterised, not to misplaced emphasis driving its popularity — misrepresents the argument.","error_type":"misrepresents_focus"},"D":{"fits":false,"reason":"The comparison given in this option cannot be inferred from the passage — the passage doesn't compare Stoicism to other philosophies on this specific point.","error_type":"out_of_scope"}},"common_errors":{"A":"too_general","C":"misrepresents_focus","D":"out_of_scope"},"difficulty":"medium","explanation":"The passage discusses the misconceptions surrounding Stoicism, a philosophy often misunderstood as promoting passive endurance of life’s difficulties without achieving anything more. It argues that this view overlooks Stoicism’s potential to provide lasting peace and tranquillity, which is more profound than commonly perceived.\\nOption A: incorrect because while the passage mentions that Stoicism is “not more popular,” it focuses more on why it is misunderstood rather than the lack of attention. This option is too general and does not capture the nuance of the passage.\\nOption B: correct because this option correctly identifies the core argument: the passage critiques how Stoicism is perceived as merely about “endurance” rather than offering deeper promises of peace and transcendence.\\nOption C: incorrect because the passage states that Stoicism is “not more popular” and attributes this to its being mischaracterized, not to any misplaced emphasis driving its popularity. This option misrepresents the argument.\\nOption D: incorrect because the comparison given in this option cannot be inferred.\\nThus, the correct option is Choice (B)","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"instructions":"Directions for questions 1 to 30: Four alternative summaries are given below each text. Choose the option that best captures the essence of the text."},{"id":"BE_E7_019","exercise":"exercise_7","type":"best_essence","passage":"At its most extreme, Decadence is a reckless pursuit of intense sensations: one that often teeters on the brink of scandal and sometimes topples over. When Dorian Gray was used as evidence against its author in the decade's most sensational trial in 1895, there was a moral backlash against the so-called 'Decadent movement'. In recent decades, the term decadence has been appropriated as a marketing pitch to describe purchasable moments of self-indulgence – a box of chocolate truffles or a spa afternoon at a mid-price chain hotel. However, as it has become a consumer product, decadence has been shorn of its very modern questions about the place of pleasure in our lives and its potential to change our values forever, and these are worth asking.","options":{"A":"Decadence has transformed from immorality to a consumer product of pleasure, losing its philosophical edge and raising questions about its potential to challenge societal values.","B":"Decadence has been present throughout human history either as a moral relaxation or an effort to seek pleasure regardless of moral implications that might change societal values.","C":"Just as in the case of the author of Dorian Gray, decadence has always faced social stigma and scrutiny because of the moral implications it raises.","D":"Decadence is the extreme case of pleasure seeking, which evokes moral recklessness and attracts societal rejection due to its unwanted effects on society."},"correct_answer":"A","correct_answer_value":"Decadence has transformed from immorality to a consumer product of pleasure, losing its philosophical edge and raising questions about its potential to challenge societal values.","option_analysis":{"A":{"fits":true,"reason":"Mirrors the passage's message — decadence transformed from reckless sensations to a consumer product, losing its philosophical depth, and raises questions about pleasure and values."},"B":{"fits":false,"reason":"Misrepresents the passage by focusing on decadence's historical presence — the passage focuses specifically on the recent commercialization and philosophical loss.","error_type":"misrepresents_focus"},"C":{"fits":false,"reason":"Only partially addresses the passage — focuses only on social stigma, ignoring the broader theme of decadence's transformation to consumerism.","error_type":"incomplete"},"D":{"fits":false,"reason":"Doesn't capture the full range of ideas — particularly the shift to consumerism and the loss of philosophical depth which are central to the passage.","error_type":"incomplete"}},"common_errors":{"B":"misrepresents_focus","C":"incomplete","D":"incomplete"},"difficulty":"hard","explanation":"The passage discusses the transformation of the concept of decadence, which was originally associated with moral recklessness and scandalous behaviour, into a consumer product that focuses on self-indulgence. Over time, decadence has lost its philosophical depth regarding pleasure and its potential to challenge societal values, becoming more of a consumer commodity, like chocolate truffles or spa days.\\nOption A: correct because it closely mirrors the passage’s message. The passage discusses how decadence has transformed from being associated with “reckless pursuit of intense sensations” and “immorality” to a “consumer product of pleasure.” It also highlights the loss of its philosophical depth, and while it doesn’t say explicitly that this transformation raises questions about its potential to challenge societal values, it does emphasize that the “questions about the place of pleasure in our lives” are still worth considering.\\nOption B: incorrect because it misrepresents the passage’s focus on recent commercialization and philosophical loss.\\nOption C: incorrect because it only partially addresses the passage’s broader theme of decadence’s transformation.\\nOption D: incorrect because it doesn’t capture the full range of ideas presented in the passage, particularly the shift to consumerism and the loss of philosophical depth.\\nThus, the correct option is Choice (A)","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"instructions":"Directions for questions 1 to 30: Four alternative summaries are given below each text. Choose the option that best captures the essence of the text."},{"id":"BE_E7_020","exercise":"exercise_7","type":"best_essence","passage":"Global average levels of carbon dioxide in the atmosphere temporarily broke the 400 parts per million level earlier this year for the first time. The world has now warmed by 1 °C relative to pre-industrial times. Climate models show that if CO2 levels stopped rising now, the world would still warm by a further 0.6 °C. This means that even if global emissions were cut by 60 per cent now, which is what it would take to stabilise CO2 levels, we would still hit 1.6 °C of warming. If emissions stopped altogether, CO2 levels would soon fall. That would lead to a smaller eventual temperature rise. Unfortunately, even if countries stick to climate agreements, emissions and CO2 levels will continue to climb well past 2030.","options":{"A":"Emission control, while consequential in the short term, is not likely to provide a long-term solution for the climate change problem.","B":"Short-term emission reductions alone will not immediately solve climate change but are necessary to stabilize CO2 levels and limit future warming.","C":"Efforts of various governments to undo the climate change are not likely to come to fruition because of constraints in controlling emissions.","D":"The extent of global warming depends on the emissions currently being released, especially those of CO2."},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"Short-term emission reductions alone will not immediately solve climate change but are necessary to stabilize CO2 levels and limit future warming.","option_analysis":{"A":{"fits":false,"reason":"The passage does not suggest emission control is only a short-term measure or irrelevant in the long term — emission control is emphasised as crucial for stabilising CO2 levels.","error_type":"misrepresents_focus"},"B":{"fits":true,"reason":"Accurately reflects the nuanced message — emission reductions won't have immediate effects but are essential for long-term climate stabilisation."},"C":{"fits":false,"reason":"The passage acknowledges emissions will continue past 2030, but does not outright claim efforts will fail entirely — overstates the pessimism.","error_type":"misrepresents_focus"},"D":{"fits":false,"reason":"While partially correct, this option does not address the delayed effects of CO2 already in the atmosphere and the role of future emission reductions — too narrow.","error_type":"too_narrow"}},"common_errors":{"A":"misrepresents_focus","C":"misrepresents_focus","D":"too_narrow"},"difficulty":"hard","explanation":"The passage explains the challenges of addressing climate change by reducing carbon dioxide (CO2) emissions. It highlights that even significant reductions in emissions (such as 60%) will not immediately stop global warming due to the delayed effects of CO2 already in the atmosphere. However, stopping emissions entirely could eventually limit the extent of warming. Unfortunately, emissions are expected to continue rising despite climate agreements.\\nOption A: Incorrect. The passage does not suggest that emission control is only a short-term measure or irrelevant in the long term. On the contrary, it emphasizes that cutting emissions is crucial to stabilizing CO2 levels and mitigating future warming. This option misrepresents the role of emission control.\\nOption B: Correct. This option accurately reflects the nuanced message of the passage, emphasizing that while emission reductions won’t have immediate effects, they are essential for long-term climate stabilization and limiting additional warming.\\nOption C: Incorrect. The passage acknowledges the likelihood of emissions continuing past 2030, but it does not outright claim that efforts will fail entirely. Moreover, it does not focus on governments’ constraints but rather on the inherent lag in climate response to emissions reductions.\\nOption D: incorrect because while it is partially correct, it does focus on the delayed effects of CO2 already in the atmosphere and the role of future emission reductions.\\nThus, the correct option is Choice (B)","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"instructions":"Directions for questions 1 to 30: Four alternative summaries are given below each text. Choose the option that best captures the essence of the text."},{"id":"BE_E7_021","exercise":"exercise_7","type":"best_essence","passage":"Alongside equality, freedom and opportunity, fear has long played a powerful role in political discourse. In ordinary life, fear is often a fitting response to danger. If you encounter a snake while out on a hike, fear will lead you to back away and exercise caution. If the snake is poisonous, fear will have saved your life. By contrast, the fears that dominate political discourse are less concrete. We are told to fear elites, terrorists, religious zealots, godless atheists, sexists, feminists, Marxists and the enemies of democracy. Yet even as these purported poisons are less obviously lethal, political rhetoricians have long understood that making them salient is a powerful way to shape citizens' motivations.","options":{"A":"While fear is a natural and protective response to immediate dangers in everyday life, political discourse often manipulates abstract fears—such as those of various social or political groups—to influence and shape citizens' motivations, even when these fears are not directly threatening.","B":"In political discourse, fear is often used to influence people's behaviour by focusing on abstract threats, such as political ideologies or social movements, even when these fears are not immediately dangerous to individuals.","C":"While fear is a useful tool for survival in everyday life, political discourse manipulates these pernicious fears to create fear of elites, terrorists, and social movements, making them central to shaping public opinions and motivations.","D":"Fear, when experienced in ordinary life, is always rational and life-saving, but political discourse often overemphasizes these fears to create unnecessary divisions between people and groups, making them a central part of political discussions."},"correct_answer":"A","correct_answer_value":"While fear is a natural and protective response to immediate dangers in everyday life, political discourse often manipulates abstract fears—such as those of various social or political groups—to influence and shape citizens' motivations, even when these fears are not directly threatening.","option_analysis":{"A":{"fits":true,"reason":"Appropriately captures both the natural protective role of fear in everyday life and the manipulation of abstract fears in political discourse to shape motivations."},"B":{"fits":false,"reason":"Lacks the nuanced connection to the idea of shaping citizens' motivations as emphasised in the original text — omits the contrast with everyday fear.","error_type":"incomplete"},"C":{"fits":false,"reason":"Focuses more on the manipulation of fear without emphasising the contrast between everyday life and political discourse — misses the structural contrast.","error_type":"incomplete"},"D":{"fits":false,"reason":"Overemphasizes the rationality of everyday fear and introduces the idea of unnecessary divisions — the passage does not make this point about divisions.","error_type":"out_of_scope"}},"common_errors":{"B":"incomplete","C":"incomplete","D":"out_of_scope"},"difficulty":"medium","explanation":"Option A: Correct. This option appropriately captures the essence of the text. The text contrasts the natural, protective role of fear in everyday life (e.g., fear of a snake) with the manipulation of more abstract fears (e.g., fear of various social and political groups) in political discourse. Option A directly highlights this distinction, pointing out that fear in everyday life is protective, whereas political discourse manipulates more abstract fears, which may not be directly threatening but still influence people’s motivations.\\nOption B: While it mentions abstract threats, it lacks the nuanced connection to the idea of shaping citizens’ motivations as emphasized in the original text.\\nOption C: Focuses more on the manipulation of fear without emphasizing the contrast between everyday life and political discourse.\\nOption D: Overemphasizes the rationality of everyday fear and introduces the idea of unnecessary divisions, which isn’t the central focus of the text. Choice (A)","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"instructions":"Directions for questions 1 to 30: Four alternative summaries are given below each text. Choose the option that best captures the essence of the text."},{"id":"BE_E7_022","exercise":"exercise_7","type":"best_essence","passage":"Learning requires that a student place trust in a teacher, or in an authoritative text, without yet knowing if the trust is warranted. One has to trust that the teacher knows what he is talking about, or that the text contains riches that are not yet visible through a thicket of strangeness and obscurity. The necessity of trust in education is not much appreciated because it sits uncomfortably with our public creed of individualism. Individualism tacitly posits a kind of epistemic self-sufficiency that everyone has by default, or can achieve simply by following a clearly stated method of reasoning ('critical thinking skills'), applied to 'information' that is readily available. This flattens the hierarchical relationship between student and teacher, or between student and text, and such flattening is one instance of our fraught relationship to the idea of authority.","options":{"A":"Learning depends on the student's ability to trust only verified teachers or texts, which contrasts with individualism's focus on self-sufficiency.","B":"Education requires a student to rely entirely on the teacher's authority, as individualism suggests that independent reasoning alone cannot lead to knowledge.","C":"The student's trust in a teacher or text should only be given after assessing their reliability, challenging the individualistic belief in personal autonomy in learning.","D":"Learning requires a fundamental trust in teachers or texts, which conflicts with the individualistic belief in self-sufficiency and challenges traditional views on authority."},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"Learning requires a fundamental trust in teachers or texts, which conflicts with the individualistic belief in self-sufficiency and challenges traditional views on authority.","option_analysis":{"A":{"fits":false,"reason":"Implies students should trust only verified teachers or texts — the passage argues trust is needed before verification is possible.","error_type":"contradicts_passage"},"B":{"fits":false,"reason":"States students must 'rely entirely' on teacher's authority and dismisses independent reasoning — the passage does not make either of these extreme claims.","error_type":"misrepresents_focus"},"C":{"fits":false,"reason":"Suggests trust should be given only after assessing reliability — the passage argues the opposite: trust is needed initially, without prior assessment.","error_type":"contradicts_passage"},"D":{"fits":true,"reason":"Captures both key points — trust in teachers/texts is fundamental to learning, and this conflicts with individualism's belief in self-sufficiency."}},"common_errors":{"A":"contradicts_passage","B":"misrepresents_focus","C":"contradicts_passage"},"difficulty":"hard","explanation":"The passage emphasizes that learning depends on a foundational trust in the teacher or text, which may initially seem obscure or unfamiliar. This reliance is necessary even if the student cannot yet verify the teacher’s or text’s reliability. The passage contrasts this need for trust with the individualistic belief in “epistemic self-sufficiency,” where individuals assume they can rely on independent reasoning and readily accessible information. The passage goes on to suggest that individualism’s preference for self-reliance undermines the traditional hierarchical nature of education, where authority plays a crucial role. Option D best captures these key points by highlighting both the trust in authority required for learning and the tension this creates with individualism.\\nChoice A: This option implies that students should only place trust in “verified” teachers or texts. However, the passage indicates that students need to trust even before they have verified the reliability of the teacher or text. The passage underscores that trust is necessary “without yet knowing if the trust is warranted”.\\nChoice B: This option suggests an extreme reliance on the teacher’s authority (“rely entirely”) and implies that independent reasoning is incapable of leading to knowledge. The passage, however, does not state that students must “rely entirely” on authority, nor does it dismiss independent reasoning altogether. Hence this option is a distortion of what is stated in the passage.\\nChoice C: This option suggests that students should assess the reliability of teachers or texts before placing trust in them (“trust… only be given after assessing their reliability”). However, the passage argues the opposite: that trust is needed initially, even when the reliability of the teacher or text is not yet clear. Hence, this option runs contrary to what is stated in the passage.\\nChoice D: Correct based on the explanation given at the beginning. Choice (D)","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"instructions":"Directions for questions 1 to 30: Four alternative summaries are given below each text. Choose the option that best captures the essence of the text."},{"id":"BE_E7_023","exercise":"exercise_7","type":"best_essence","passage":"Life is often defined by what we get right. It is explained by growth, replication and adaptation to the environment. But mistakes are everywhere. A theory of mistakes will help us understand, in a systematic and experimentally driven way, behaviour that threatens the flourishing of living beings. It will also help us appreciate the normativity that runs through life. While some still view 'teleology' with scepticism, mistake theory may well be the antidote that challenges conventional wisdom about the goals of living things. In the intricate biological dance of right and wrong, we might just find the key to understanding the deeper purposes that drive life on Earth.","options":{"A":"A theory of mistakes could provide deeper insights into life's goals by systematically examining behaviours that hinder flourishing, challenging traditional views on purpose and teleology.","B":"A theory of mistakes focuses on how errors define life by overshadowing growth and adaptation, revealing the futility of seeking purpose or teleology in biological processes.","C":"Mistakes are a minor aspect of life compared to growth and replication, and their study is unlikely to offer substantial insights into understanding life's deeper purposes or goals.","D":"While life's growth and adaptation are driven by avoiding mistakes, a theory of errors primarily seeks to eliminate behaviours that threaten flourishing rather than exploring deeper goals."},"correct_answer":"A","correct_answer_value":"A theory of mistakes could provide deeper insights into life's goals by systematically examining behaviours that hinder flourishing, challenging traditional views on purpose and teleology.","option_analysis":{"A":{"fits":true,"reason":"Accurately reflects the focus on a theory of mistakes providing insights into life's goals, systematically examining hindering behaviours, and challenging scepticism about teleology."},"B":{"fits":false,"reason":"Misrepresents the passage — claims mistakes overshadow growth and suggests teleology is futile, while the passage advocates for the significance of mistakes in understanding purpose.","error_type":"contradicts_passage"},"C":{"fits":false,"reason":"Minimises the role of mistakes — directly contradicts the passage's emphasis on their importance in gaining insights into life's purposes.","error_type":"contradicts_passage"},"D":{"fits":false,"reason":"Oversimplifies the theory as merely seeking to eliminate harmful behaviours — ignores its role in exploring life's deeper goals and purposes.","error_type":"incomplete"}},"common_errors":{"B":"contradicts_passage","C":"contradicts_passage","D":"incomplete"},"difficulty":"hard","explanation":"Choice A: Correct. The passage discusses how life is often defined by success but is also profoundly influenced by mistakes. It emphasizes that a “theory of mistakes” can provide a systematic understanding of behaviours that threaten the flourishing of life while offering new perspectives on life’s goals and purpose, particularly through challenging scepticism about teleology (the study of purpose in life). Option (A) accurately reflects this focus by highlighting: 1. The role of a theory of mistakes in providing insights into life’s goals. 2. The idea of systematically examining behaviours that hinder flourishing. 3. The notion of challenging traditional views on purpose and teleology, which is explicitly mentioned in the passage.\\nChoice B: misrepresents the passage by claiming that mistakes overshadow growth and adaptation and suggesting that teleology is futile, while the passage advocates for the significance of mistakes in understanding purpose and challenging scepticism toward teleology.\\nChoice C: minimizes the role of mistakes, contradicting the passage’s emphasis on their importance in gaining insights into life’s purposes.\\nChoice D: oversimplifies the theory as merely seeking to eliminate harmful behaviours, ignoring its role in exploring life’s deeper goals and purposes. Choice (A)","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"instructions":"Directions for questions 1 to 30: Four alternative summaries are given below each text. Choose the option that best captures the essence of the text."},{"id":"BE_E7_024","exercise":"exercise_7","type":"best_essence","passage":"Anthropological and cultural psychological studies have long suggested that wisdom is deeply embedded within specific cultural norms and values. Many researchers have emphasised the differences between 'Eastern' and 'Western' conceptions of wisdom. The presumed collectivism of Chinese culture, for example, is often attributed to the Confucian and Taoist traditions, which place great importance on social and contextual awareness. In contrast, the individualism of Western cultures is frequently linked to a focus on analytical thinking coming from ancient Greek and Roman philosophers, as well as the intellectual ideals of the Enlightenment. Consequently, it seemed straightforward to assume that the socio-emotional awareness dimension we identified would be more closely associated with wisdom by participants in the global East whereas the reflective orientation dimension would be prioritised by those in the West.","options":{"A":"Wisdom is universally understood across cultures, with both Eastern and Western traditions equally emphasizing the importance of socio-emotional awareness and reflective thinking.","B":"Wisdom is shaped by cultural norms, with Eastern traditions emphasizing socio-emotional awareness rooted in collectivism and Western traditions prioritizing reflective thinking linked to individualism and analytical reasoning.","C":"The individualism of Western cultures, influenced by ancient Greek, Roman, and Enlightenment thought, contrasts with Eastern collectivism, but both cultural traditions prioritize analytical thinking as a cornerstone of wisdom.","D":"Research into cultural psychology has shown that while collectivist cultures, like those in the East, value socio-emotional awareness, reflective thinking is equally emphasized across both Eastern and Western societies."},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"Wisdom is shaped by cultural norms, with Eastern traditions emphasizing socio-emotional awareness rooted in collectivism and Western traditions prioritizing reflective thinking linked to individualism and analytical reasoning.","option_analysis":{"A":{"fits":false,"reason":"Incorrectly claims wisdom is universally understood and that both traditions equally emphasize socio-emotional and reflective thinking — contradicts the passage's argument about distinct cultural perspectives.","error_type":"contradicts_passage"},"B":{"fits":true,"reason":"Directly reflects the passage's distinction — Eastern wisdom with socio-emotional awareness and collectivism; Western wisdom with reflective thinking and individualism."},"C":{"fits":false,"reason":"Falsely asserts that both traditions prioritize analytical thinking — analytical thinking is specifically linked to Western cultures in the passage.","error_type":"factual_error"},"D":{"fits":false,"reason":"Incorrectly suggests that reflective thinking is equally emphasized in both cultures — the passage specifically associates it with the West.","error_type":"factual_error"}},"common_errors":{"A":"contradicts_passage","C":"factual_error","D":"factual_error"},"difficulty":"medium","explanation":"The passage clearly distinguishes between Eastern and Western conceptions of wisdom, associating Eastern wisdom with socio-emotional awareness and collectivism, and Western wisdom with reflective thinking and individualism. Option B directly reflects this division while capturing the key points regarding cultural influence on wisdom.\\nChoice A: incorrectly claims that wisdom is universally understood across cultures and equally emphasizes socio-emotional awareness and reflective thinking, which contradicts the passage’s argument about distinct cultural perspectives.\\nChoice B: Correct based on the explanation given at the beginning.\\nChoice C: falsely asserts that both traditions prioritize analytical thinking, which is not true according to the passage, as analytical thinking is more closely linked to Western cultures.\\nChoice D: incorrectly suggests that reflective thinking is equally emphasized in both cultures, which the passage does not imply. It specifically associates reflective thinking with the West and socio-emotional awareness with the East.\\nChoice (B)","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"instructions":"Directions for questions 1 to 30: Four alternative summaries are given below each text. Choose the option that best captures the essence of the text."},{"id":"BE_E7_025","exercise":"exercise_7","type":"best_essence","passage":"Many of the longest-enduring human cultures have ancestral knowledge that in some cases is several hundred years old and has undoubtedly been key to their survival. Countless anthropologists and archaeologists have shown that human societies can retain knowledge for centuries, pointing to the reciting of genealogies as a common example. But survival requires more than lists. It requires people to understand their deep geographical and historical context, their place in the world and how to sustain it. I suggest that our distant ancestors and their societies were no less risk-aware and risk-averse than we are today. Their underlying goal was for their bloodline to endure, which is essentially why today we educate young people, teaching them reason, giving them an understanding of the world they inhabit so they might survive. Reason does not depend on books but on acquiring and processing knowledge from whatever sources are available and are best aligned with what we need to know in order to survive.","options":{"A":"Ancestral knowledge, including genealogies, is primarily valuable for preserving cultural traditions, with little connection to the survival of human societies in terms of risk awareness or understanding the world.","B":"Human societies prioritize preserving genealogical records above all else, with education focusing mainly on analytical reasoning and academic knowledge to ensure survival, similar to the practices of our ancestors.","C":"Survival is achieved through the accumulation of lists and facts, with little regard for understanding the broader historical and geographical context, and modern education is based solely on the importance of books and formal learning.","D":"Human societies have preserved ancestral knowledge, which has been essential for survival, with an emphasis on understanding geographical, historical, and contextual factors, much like how education today imparts knowledge to ensure future survival."},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"Human societies have preserved ancestral knowledge, which has been essential for survival, with an emphasis on understanding geographical, historical, and contextual factors, much like how education today imparts knowledge to ensure future survival.","option_analysis":{"A":{"fits":false,"reason":"Misrepresents the passage by focusing too narrowly on genealogies and omitting the broader point about understanding geographical and historical context for survival.","error_type":"misrepresents_focus"},"B":{"fits":false,"reason":"Wrongly suggests the primary focus is on preserving genealogies and analytical reasoning — overlooks the passage's emphasis on understanding the world and processing knowledge from various sources.","error_type":"misrepresents_focus"},"C":{"fits":false,"reason":"Inaccurately suggests survival relies on accumulating lists and facts, and that modern education depends solely on books — directly contradicts the passage.","error_type":"contradicts_passage"},"D":{"fits":true,"reason":"Correctly highlights ancestral knowledge's role in survival, the emphasis on understanding context, and the parallel with modern education."}},"common_errors":{"A":"misrepresents_focus","B":"misrepresents_focus","C":"contradicts_passage"},"difficulty":"medium","explanation":"The passage discusses how ancestral knowledge has been key to survival, with a focus on understanding the environment and historical context, and relates this to the importance of education today in preparing individuals for survival. Option B captures these points well, highlighting the connection between ancestral knowledge and modern education.\\nChoice A: misrepresents the passage by focusing too narrowly on genealogies and omitting the broader point about the need for understanding geographical and historical context to ensure survival.\\nChoice B: wrongly suggests that the primary focus of human societies and modern education is preserving genealogies and analytical reasoning, which overlooks the passage’s emphasis on understanding the world and processing knowledge from various sources, not just formal education.\\nChoice C: inaccurately suggests that survival only relies on accumulating lists and facts, disregarding the passage’s emphasis on understanding one’s context and processing knowledge from multiple sources, rather than solely relying on books.\\nChoice D: Correct based on the explanation given at the beginning. Choice (D)","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"instructions":"Directions for questions 1 to 30: Four alternative summaries are given below each text. Choose the option that best captures the essence of the text."},{"id":"BE_E7_026","exercise":"exercise_7","type":"best_essence","passage":"The British colonisers who travelled to India from the 18th century onwards were steeped in the Classics; they knew their Greek and Latin (if not the languages of India) and quoted liberally from Horace and Virgil. Not all Britons, of course – but it was not a small contingent who took ancient Greece and Rome with them to the subcontinent. In part, Classics was an affectation; speaking Latin was a sign that you were polished, well read and sophisticated, and acting like a gentleman was important to English self-regard during the Raj. But connections with Greece and Rome went deeper: the British saw their actions in India through the lens of their classical educations and understood their decisions in the light of Graeco-Roman antiquity. For some, the epic poems of Homer bore startling similarities with Sanskrit epics and were a basis for cultural comparison; for others, Virgil's Aeneid offered guidance on how to be imperial and civilised at the same time.","options":{"A":"The British colonisers in India used their knowledge of Greek and Latin primarily as a tool for governance, focusing on administrative efficiency rather than cultural or imperial self-justification.","B":"Classical education among British colonisers served only as an affectation to signal sophistication, with little influence on their actions or understanding of Indian culture during the Raj.","C":"The British colonisers in India, influenced by their classical education in Greek and Latin, drew on Graeco-Roman antiquity to shape their self-image, justify their actions, and find cultural parallels with Indian traditions.","D":"The British colonisers in India dismissed Indian traditions as inferior, relying solely on Graeco-Roman ideals to define their sense of imperialism and civilisation."},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"The British colonisers in India, influenced by their classical education in Greek and Latin, drew on Graeco-Roman antiquity to shape their self-image, justify their actions, and find cultural parallels with Indian traditions.","option_analysis":{"A":{"fits":false,"reason":"Distorts the passage by emphasising governance and administrative efficiency — the passage focuses on cultural comparisons and self-justification, not administrative efficiency.","error_type":"misrepresents_focus"},"B":{"fits":false,"reason":"Reduces classical education to a superficial affectation — overlooks the deeper influence of Graeco-Roman antiquity on colonisers' decisions and cultural comparisons.","error_type":"incomplete"},"C":{"fits":true,"reason":"Integrates all key points — classical education, shaping self-image, justifying actions, and cultural parallels with Indian traditions."},"D":{"fits":false,"reason":"Misrepresents the passage — the passage explicitly mentions cultural comparisons such as between Homeric and Sanskrit epics; the British did not dismiss Indian traditions entirely.","error_type":"contradicts_passage"}},"common_errors":{"A":"misrepresents_focus","B":"incomplete","D":"contradicts_passage"},"difficulty":"medium","explanation":"Option C is the correct answer. The passage highlights the role of classical education among British colonisers in India, explaining how their knowledge of Greek and Latin not only served as a marker of sophistication but also influenced their perceptions, decisions, and cultural comparisons with Indian traditions. This option succinctly integrates these key points.\\nChoice A: distorts the passage by emphasizing governance and administrative efficiency, which is not mentioned as a primary outcome of their classical education. Instead, the passage focuses on cultural comparisons and self-justification.\\nChoice B: overlooks the deeper influence of Graeco-Roman antiquity on the colonisers’ decisions and cultural comparisons, reducing classical education to a superficial affectation.\\nChoice C: Correct based on the explanation given at the beginning.\\nChoice D: misrepresents the passage by suggesting that the British dismissed Indian traditions entirely, whereas the passage explicitly mentions cultural comparisons, such as between Homeric and Sanskrit epics. Choice (C)","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"instructions":"Directions for questions 1 to 30: Four alternative summaries are given below each text. Choose the option that best captures the essence of the text."},{"id":"BE_E7_027","exercise":"exercise_7","type":"best_essence","passage":"The ancient Greek root of the word comedy is disputed, but most scholars agree that it combines the word ōidē, meaning song or ode, with kōmos, meaning release or revelry. Alternatively, the root of comedy is said to combine ōidē with the similar-sounding word kome, meaning village or community. Kōmos also has a ritual element, in the sense of license to speak truth to power and to settle scores in public. But in the ancient Greek context, as in many others, that license was not issued to all of the citizens all of the time. And until recently, no one dreamed that billions of human beings would amuse themselves by raging at unseen enemies through a medium that profits from cranking up the crazy.","options":{"A":"The origins of the word comedy reflect ideas of song, revelry, and community, with its ancient Greek roots tied to rituals of truth-telling and public expression, contrasting sharply with modern practices of online outrage.","B":"The ancient Greek roots of comedy emphasize the ideas of communal song and truth-telling, which were accessible to all citizens at all times and have evolved into modern platforms for free expression.","C":"Comedy, rooted in ancient Greek rituals of release and revelry, has always been a medium for unrestricted public expression, showing continuity from its origins to modern online outrage.","D":"The etymology of comedy, tied to Greek words for song and community, reflects a tradition of communal harmony and truth-telling that remains largely unchanged in contemporary forms of entertainment."},"correct_answer":"A","correct_answer_value":"The origins of the word comedy reflect ideas of song, revelry, and community, with its ancient Greek roots tied to rituals of truth-telling and public expression, contrasting sharply with modern practices of online outrage.","option_analysis":{"A":{"fits":true,"reason":"Captures both the historical context and the modern shift — ancient roots of song, revelry, truth-telling, and the contrast with modern online outrage."},"B":{"fits":false,"reason":"Inaccurately states truth-telling was accessible to all citizens at all times — the passage explicitly states that license was NOT issued to all citizens all of the time.","error_type":"contradicts_passage"},"C":{"fits":false,"reason":"Incorrectly implies comedy has always been about unrestricted expression — the passage notes it was controlled and ritualistic, contrasting with modern outrage.","error_type":"contradicts_passage"},"D":{"fits":false,"reason":"Misleadingly suggests communal harmony and truth-telling are consistent with modern forms — ignores the passage's clear distinction between ancient practices and today's chaotic online behaviour.","error_type":"contradicts_passage"}},"common_errors":{"B":"contradicts_passage","C":"contradicts_passage","D":"contradicts_passage"},"difficulty":"hard","explanation":"Choice A: Correct. The passage traces the disputed etymology of “comedy” and explores its ritualistic and communal origins in ancient Greece, where it involved limited public license for truth-telling and expression. It contrasts this with the modern phenomenon of widespread online anger for amusement. This summary captures the key elements of both the historical context and the modern shift.\\nChoice B: inaccurately states that truth-telling was accessible to all citizens at all times, ignoring the passage’s emphasis that such license was limited in ancient Greece.\\nChoice C: incorrectly implies that comedy has always been about unrestricted expression, while the passage notes that it was controlled and ritualistic in ancient Greece, contrasting this with modern online outrage.\\nChoice D: misleadingly suggests that the communal harmony and truth-telling of ancient comedy are consistent with modern forms, ignoring the passage’s clear distinction between ancient practices and today’s chaotic online behaviour. Choice (A)","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"instructions":"Directions for questions 1 to 30: Four alternative summaries are given below each text. Choose the option that best captures the essence of the text."},{"id":"BE_E7_028","exercise":"exercise_7","type":"best_essence","passage":"Pop music and literature are two separate miracles, the silent shout and the screamed secret, two wonders working to their own, different and divided rules. Each has seductive thrills of its own. Pop music has no need to attain the form of literature to achieve greatness. A great many of its practitioners have thought otherwise however, and there have been countless pretenders of one form to the other. Whether its Iron Maiden raiding Coleridge or The Eurythmics mugging 1984, the straightforward homage, sad to say, usually rings false. Frank Zappa's denunciation of rock music writing was 'like dancing about architecture' and the 'category error' is just as stark the other way. The essence of one does not easily translate into the other. That doesn't mean it is not possible however, that the breadth, sway, richness and ambiguity of literature cannot be captured in song. A true – successful – literary sensibility in pop music is a rare thing indeed, but it can happen. It doesn't come from showboating references but a much deeper understanding of the texture of literature.","options":{"A":"Pop music and literature are fundamentally separate art forms, and any attempts to combine them often fail, as seen in examples like Iron Maiden and The Eurythmics.","B":"Despite being distinct art forms with separate rules, pop music and literature occasionally achieve greatness by borrowing from one another, showcasing their shared potential.","C":"Although pop music and literature are entirely different forms of art, superficial imitations fail, while a nuanced understanding of literature can occasionally elevate pop music.","D":"Pop music and literature, while distinct in essence, can occasionally intersect meaningfully when the deep texture of literature is captured in music, rather than through superficial references."},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"Pop music and literature, while distinct in essence, can occasionally intersect meaningfully when the deep texture of literature is captured in music, rather than through superficial references.","option_analysis":{"A":{"fits":false,"reason":"Overemphasises failure and ignores the passage's mention that meaningful integration is possible — too pessimistic.","error_type":"incomplete"},"B":{"fits":false,"reason":"Misinterprets the passage — suggests pop music and literature frequently borrow from each other, contradicting the text's emphasis on their fundamental separation and the rarity of success.","error_type":"misrepresents_focus"},"C":{"fits":false,"reason":"While it acknowledges distinct forms and failure through superficiality, it does not explicitly focus on the possibility of rare meaningful integration which is the essence of the passage.","error_type":"incomplete"},"D":{"fits":true,"reason":"Best captures the essence — acknowledges distinctness, rarity of successful integration, and that success lies in capturing the deeper texture of literature rather than superficial references."}},"common_errors":{"A":"incomplete","B":"misrepresents_focus","C":"incomplete"},"difficulty":"hard","explanation":"Choice A: This option overemphasizes the failure of attempts to combine the two forms and ignores the possibility of meaningful integration mentioned in the passage.\\nChoice B: This option misinterprets the passage by suggesting pop music and literature frequently achieve greatness by borrowing from each other, which contradicts the text’s emphasis on their fundamental separation and the rarity of success.\\nChoice C: While this option acknowledges the distinct nature of the forms and mentions failure through superficiality, it does not explicitly focus on the possibility of rare, meaningful integration, which is the essence of the passage.\\nChoice D: Correct. This option best captures the essence of the passage. It acknowledges the distinct nature of pop music and literature, emphasizes the rarity of successful integration, and highlights that such success lies in capturing the “deeper texture” of literature rather than in superficial references. Choice (D)","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"instructions":"Directions for questions 1 to 30: Four alternative summaries are given below each text. Choose the option that best captures the essence of the text."},{"id":"BE_E7_029","exercise":"exercise_7","type":"best_essence","passage":"Inoculation theory rests on a flawed portrait of human beings as passive and credulous. Far from being gullible victims of mind viruses that plague our information ecosystems, we are sophisticated agents with complex goals and identities navigating environments dominated by largely reliable information, at least on simple factual matters. To the extent that people are misinformed on these matters, it is often not because they have been duped by encounters with unreliable sources; it is because they are overly sceptical of reliable sources that contradict their preexisting beliefs, or because the consumption and propagation of misinformation promotes their interests or goals.","options":{"A":"Inoculation theory oversimplifies human behaviour, portraying people as gullible victims, whereas they are complex agents who often reject reliable information due to preexisting beliefs or personal interests.","B":"Inoculation theory's depiction of humans as susceptible to misinformation is far from incorrect, as humans often fall victim to unreliable sources in the information ecosystem.","C":"Human beings are neither passive nor gullible; misinformation spreads not due to naivety but because people reject trustworthy sources that conflict with their beliefs or serve their personal goals.","D":"Misinformation dominates our information ecosystem because humans are easily deceived, a point underscored by inoculation theory's depiction of passive gullibility."},"correct_answer":"A","correct_answer_value":"Inoculation theory oversimplifies human behaviour, portraying people as gullible victims, whereas they are complex agents who often reject reliable information due to preexisting beliefs or personal interests.","option_analysis":{"A":{"fits":true,"reason":"Accurately captures the critique of inoculation theory for depicting humans as gullible and explains that misinformation spreads due to scepticism of reliable sources or self-serving motives."},"B":{"fits":false,"reason":"Agrees with inoculation theory — the passage explicitly critiques this theory as flawed.","error_type":"contradicts_passage"},"C":{"fits":false,"reason":"Though partially correct, it fails to mention the flawed portrayal of humans in inoculation theory — omits the critique of the theory which is central to the passage.","error_type":"incomplete"},"D":{"fits":false,"reason":"Reinforces the notion of human gullibility — directly contradicts the passage's argument against passive gullibility.","error_type":"contradicts_passage"}},"common_errors":{"B":"contradicts_passage","C":"incomplete","D":"contradicts_passage"},"difficulty":"medium","explanation":"Choice A: Correct. This option accurately captures the essence of the passage. It critiques inoculation theory for depicting humans as gullible and explains that misinformation spreads due to scepticism of reliable sources or self-serving motives, not gullibility.\\nChoice B: Misrepresents the passage by agreeing with inoculation theory, which the passage explicitly critiques.\\nChoice C: Though this option is partially correct, it fails to mention the flawed portrayal of humans in inoculation theory, which is central to the passage.\\nChoice D: This option contradicts the passage’s argument by reinforcing the notion of human gullibility, which the passage challenges. Choice (A)","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"instructions":"Directions for questions 1 to 30: Four alternative summaries are given below each text. Choose the option that best captures the essence of the text."},{"id":"BE_E7_030","exercise":"exercise_7","type":"best_essence","passage":"Over the past few decades, turkeys which were once creatures of the countryside have found their way into urban areas. City parks and other urban green spaces offer ideal foraging grounds for them with an abundance of lush grass, insects and acorns. Cities also create edge habitats where forests meet open spaces that provide turkeys with both tree cover for roosting and spread-out areas for foraging. Cities are also relatively safe places for turkeys to live. While rural turkeys contend with predators like coyotes and bobcats, their city cousins are free from such threats. Yet, as wild turkeys settle into urban spaces, they can sometimes cause conflicts. They block traffic, raid gardens, scratch cars and even chase children, mail carriers and police — reminding their human neighbours that these bold birds are still very much wild animals, not oversized, feathered pets.","options":{"A":"Turkeys are better adapted to live in cities, which provide them with a surprising range of perks—plentiful food, prime roosting and foraging spots, and safety from predators.","B":"As many wild populations of turkeys decline, some city dwellers flourish—and may become a source for reintroductions to rural areas.","C":"Over the last few decades, wild turkeys have moved into cities, where they thrive thanks to few predators, abundant food and plenty of space to roam and roost.","D":"Wild turkeys, once rural dwellers, have adapted to urban areas due to abundant resources and safety, but their presence often leads to conflicts with humans, highlighting their untamed nature."},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"Wild turkeys, once rural dwellers, have adapted to urban areas due to abundant resources and safety, but their presence often leads to conflicts with humans, highlighting their untamed nature.","option_analysis":{"A":{"fits":false,"reason":"Captures the perks cities provide but omits the key aspect of human-wildlife conflict — incomplete summary.","error_type":"incomplete"},"B":{"fits":false,"reason":"Shifts focus by mentioning reintroductions to rural areas — this is not addressed in the passage.","error_type":"out_of_scope"},"C":{"fits":false,"reason":"Mentions the urban shift and factors helping turkeys thrive but fails to address the conflicts between turkeys and humans — omits a critical aspect.","error_type":"incomplete"},"D":{"fits":true,"reason":"Effectively combines all major points — transition from rural to urban, benefits of cities, resulting human-wildlife conflicts, and their wild nature."}},"common_errors":{"A":"incomplete","B":"out_of_scope","C":"incomplete"},"difficulty":"easy","explanation":"Choice A: While this option captures the perks that cities provide for turkeys (food, roosting spots, and safety), it omits the key aspect of human-wildlife conflict mentioned in the passage. Thus, it doesn’t fully encompass the central focus of the text.\\nChoice B: This option shifts focus by mentioning reintroductions to rural areas, which is not addressed in the passage. It strays from the main theme of urban adaptation and resulting conflicts.\\nChoice C: Though this option mentions the urban shift and factors that help turkeys thrive, it fails to address the conflicts between turkeys and humans, which is a critical aspect of the text.\\nChoice D: Correct. This option effectively combines all major points: the transition of turkeys from rural to urban areas, the benefits of cities (resources and safety), and the resulting human-wildlife conflicts, while also emphasizing their wild nature. This makes it the most comprehensive summary of the passage. Choice (D)","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"instructions":"Directions for questions 1 to 30: Four alternative summaries are given below each text. Choose the option that best captures the essence of the text."}]`);export{e as default};
